.raw\nF2007_jansenja_061.raw\nF2007_jansenja_062.raw\nF2007_jansenja_063.raw\nF2007_jansenja_064.raw\nF2007_jansenja_067.raw\nF2007_jansenja_068.raw\nF2007_jansenja_069.raw\nF2007_jansenja_070.raw\nF2007_jansenja_071.raw\nF2007_jansenja_072.raw\nF2007_jansenja_073.raw\nF2007_jansenja_074.raw\nF2007_jansenja_078.raw\nF2007_jansenja_079.raw\nF2007_jansenja_080.raw\nF2007_jansenja_081.raw\nF2007_jansenja_082.raw\nF2007_jansenja_083.raw\nF2007_jansenja_084.raw\nF2007_jansenja_085.raw\nF2007_jansenja_088.raw\nF2007_jansenja_089.raw\nF2007_jansenja_090.raw\nF2007_jansenja_091.raw\nF2007_jansenja_092.raw\nF2007_jansenja_093.raw\nF2007_jansenja_094.raw\nF2007_jansenja_095.raw\nF2007_jansenja_098.raw\nF2007_jansenja_099.raw\nF2007_jansenja_100.raw\nF2007_jansenja_101.raw\nF2007_jansenja_102.raw\nF2007_jansenja_103.raw\nF2007_jansenja_104.raw\nF2007_jansenja_105.raw\nF2007_jansenja_109.raw\nF2007_jansenja_110.raw\nF2007_jansenja_111.raw\nF2007_jansenja_112.raw\nF2007_jansenja_113.raw\nF2007_jansenja_114.raw\nF2007_jansenja_115.raw\nF2007_jansenja_116.raw\nF2007_jansenja_119.raw\nF2007_jansenja_120.raw\nF2007_jansenja_121.raw\nF2007_jansenja_122.raw\nF2007_jansenja_123.raw\nF2007_jansenja_124.raw\nF2007_jansenja_125.raw\nF2007_jansenja_126.raw\nF2007_jansenja_130.raw\nF2007_jansenja_131.raw\nF2007_jansenja_132.raw\nF2007_jansenja_133.raw\nF2007_jansenja_134.raw\nF2007_jansenja_135.raw\nF2007_jansenja_136.raw\nF2007_jansenja_137.raw\nF2007_jansenja_140.raw\nF2007_jansenja_141.raw\nF2007_jansenja_142.raw\nF2007_jansenja_143.raw\nF2007_jansenja_144.raw\nF2007_jansenja_145.raw\nF2007_jansenja_146.raw\nF2007_jansenja_147.raw\nF2007_jansenja_151.raw\nF2007_jansenja_152.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nIdentification of small molecule activators of the ubiquitin ligase E6AP/UBE3A and Angelman syndrome-derived E6AP/UBE3A variants\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nGenetic aberrations of the UBE3A gene encoding the E3 ubiquitin ligase E6AP underlie the development of Angelman syndrome (AS). Approximately 10 percent of AS individuals harbor UBE3A genes with point mutations, frequently resulting in the expression of full-length E6AP variants with defective E3 activity. Since E6AP exists in two states, an inactive and an active one, we hypothesized that distinct small molecules can stabilize the active state and that such molecules may rescue the E3 activity of AS-derived E6AP variants. Therefore, we established an assay that allows identifying modulators of E6AP in a high-throughput format. We identified several compounds that not only stimulate wild-type E6AP but also rescue the E3 activity of certain E6AP variants. Moreover, by chemical crosslinking coupled to mass spectrometry we provide evidence that the compounds stabilize an active conformation of E6AP. Thus, these compounds represent potential lead structures for the design of drugs for AS treatment.\n\n[METHODS]\nExperiments were carried out essentially as described (Sailer et al., 2018). In short, approximately 100 Î¼g of E6AP or the AS-derived E6AP variants F583S and E584Q were crosslinked by addition of H12/D12 DSS (Creative Molecules) at a ratio of 1.5 nmol / 1 Âµg protein and slight shaking for 30 min at 37 Â°C. Samples contained either 100 ÂµM of OF232, alloxazine or an equal volume of DMSO (1 % v/v). After addition of the compounds or DMSO, samples were incubated for 30 min on ice prior to crosslinking. In order to crosslink E6AP in complex with HPV-16 E6, a 1.5-fold molar excess of GST-16 E6 to E6AP was used and incubated and crosslinked under the same conditions. Proteins were crosslinked directly after purification without freezing. After quenching by addition of ammonium bicarbonate to a final concentration of 50 mM, samples were reduced, alkylated, and digested with trypsin. Digested peptides were separated from the solution and retained by a solid phase extraction system (SepPak, Waters), and then separated by size exclusion chromatography prior to liquid chromatography (LC)-MS/MS analysis on an Orbitrap Fusion Tribrid mass spectrometer (Thermo Scientific). MS measurement was performed in data-dependent acquisition mode with a cycle time of 3 s. The full scan was done in the Orbitrap with a resolution of 120,000, a scan range of 400-1500 m/z, AGC Target 2.0e5 and injection time of 50 ms. Monoisotopic precursor selection and dynamic exclusion was used for precursor selection. Only precursor charge states of 3-8 were selected for fragmentation by CID using 35 % activation energy. MS2 was carried out in the Ion Trap in normal scan range mode, AGC target 1.0e4 and injection time of 35 ms.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
